  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Remnants of Post-Tropical Cyclone Isaias to \nbring rain, breezy to windy conditions, and the threat of \ntornadoes Sunday.\n\nPost-Tropical Cyclone Isaias continues to lift northward across\nGeorgia and Alabama. This system has weakened considerably after\nlast evening's landfall while losing all tropical\ncharacteristics. The associated mid-level circulation \nstrengthens and becomes a large cutoff low later tonight. To the\neast, surface high pressure over the northeastern U.S. today \nmoves offshore off southern New England tonight.\n\nIn advance of this broad area of low pressure, a thick high\ncirrus shield continues to expand north and east into the Great\nLakes and New England. While this system still looms well to the\nsouthwest of the area, some light scattered showers continue to\nlift across I-64 toward the I-66 corridor. However, any amounts\nare very light with some radar returns not even reaching the\nground. The thick cloud cover will hold temperatures mainly in\nthe upper 60s to low 70s this afternoon.\n\nLooking ahead, the interaction of Isaias' remnants to the west \nand surface high to the east will create a large area of \nconvergence / broad frontal zone over the Mid-Atlantic. There is\ngood model agreement that rain steadily moves from south to \nnorth this evening into tonight as forcing for ascent increases \nwith some mid-level height falls in response to the approaching \ncutoff low. It will take a bit of time for the surface to truly\nsaturate, so any rainfall this afternoon to evening across \ncentral Virginia will struggle to amount to much. Eventually, \ntropical moisture with PWATs at or above 2\" surges in after \nmidnight. \n\nIt is worth mentioning again that conditions are favorable for a \nwidespread soaking rain, and 12Z and 00Z models have responded \nwith more QPF across the region. Forcing for ascent increases \ntonight, and especially Sunday morning into Sunday afternoon as \nmid-level heights fall with the approach of the cutoff low. \nEnhanced low-level convergence and moisture transport will peak\nSunday morning into early Sunday afternoon. Strengthening \neasterly flow into the Blue Ridge could also produce enhanced \nrain amounts due to orographic effects. As a result, forecast \nrain amounts have increased to 2.0-3.0\", with locally higher \namounts of 3-5\" possible. With this uptick, Flood Watches were\nissued across the Blue Ridge eastward into the central Virginia\nPiedmont region. This is in effect on Sunday 5 AM until Monday 2\nAM. Some eastward expansion may be necessary if models trends\nhold on or show further upticks.\n\nAnother noticeable trend in recent guidance is the higher \nprobabilities for severe weather Sunday afternoon. The main \nsynoptically forced rain moves north of the area early Sunday \nafternoon. Rapid height falls and increasing low-level winds \nprovide a few hour window where some breaks in the clouds area \npossible, leading to pockets of instability. A surface wave of \nlow pressure moves right through the area Sunday afternoon. \nForecast soundings indicate large, cyclonic hodographs, with \nample shear in place to support organized severe convection. \nClusters of thunderstorms develop along the leading edge of the \nsurface low pressure, with tornadoes and damaging wind gusts as \nthe main threats (looks mostly to be east of the Blue Ridge). \nThe NAM12, RRFS, and 12Z HRRR all show this scenario. The big \nquestion still remains how much instability actually develops. \nSPC has maintained a Slight Risk for Sunday, with a recent northward\nexpansion to just south of I-66 in Virginia.\n\nThe strong mid-level circulation is going to bring windy conditions \nto the Alleghenies and Blue Ridge, starting late tonight into Sunday \nmorning. Wind Advisories were issued for downsloping easterly \nwinds in the Alleghenies (western Garrett, western Grant, and \nwestern Pendleton Counties), and the Blue Ridge. The current \nforecast for peak wind gusts is 40-50 mph in the \nAlleghenies/Blue Ridge and 20-30 mph elsewhere.\n\nThe remnants of Isaias push offshore Monday, bringing an end to any \nlingering showers. While conditions return humid and warm Monday, \nthere likely will not be much in the way of shower activity due\nto subsidence behind the departing trough.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Above normal temperatures Tuesday will fall the \nsecond half of the week as moisture starved fronts move through.\n\nBrief ridging in the wake of the tropical remnants may bring high \ntemperatures Tuesday to near 80 degrees. Low pressure passing north \nof the area will send a cold front southward Tuesday night. Most of \nthe rain likely remains across Pennsylvania, although some showers \nmay sneak southward. Showers will also be possible along the \nAllegheny Front. There's still some significant model spread on the \nprogression of this front during the middle of the week, with a \nlarge spread of temperatures possible on Wednesday. However, the \ngeneral trend will be downward for the second half of the week. A \nwave of low",
